{
  "esm": "0.8.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "duo_tangent_cos",
    "description": "cos of the angle between the two local coordinate directions at a grid point, from the unit-length covariant basis of Chen (2021) C13-C14: given the point ka and its two forward neighbours kb (along the first coordinate) and kc (along the second), e1 ~ (ka x kb) x ka and e2 ~ (ka x kc) x ka, and the result is e1.e2/(|e1||e2|), which is C17. The double cross product is the neighbour PROJECTED INTO THE TANGENT PLANE at ka -- deliberately not the chord kb - ka, which is a different vector and yields a different angle (the two agree only to O(h^2): measured 2.9e-3 at N=16, 7.6e-4 at N=32, 1.9e-4 at N=64 on this grid). Because e1 and e2 are normalised, and because (ka x kb) x ka scales uniformly when all three inputs share one magnitude, the sphere radius cancels: any consistent radius may be passed. Nine scalar components in, one out -- no axis, index-set or grid knowledge, like the axis-neutral atoms under grids/latlon3d/stencils.",
    "license": "MIT",
    "tags": [
      "esd:stencil",
      "family:finite_volume",
      "grid:duo",
      "op:metric"
    ],
    "references": [
      {
        "citation": "Chen, X. (2021). The LMARS based shallow-water dynamical core on generic gnomonic cubed-sphere geometry. JAMES, 13, e2020MS002280. https://doi.org/10.1029/2020MS002280"
      }
    ]
  },
  "expression_templates": {
    "duo_tangent_cos": {
      "params": [
        "kax",
        "kay",
        "kaz",
        "kbx",
        "kby",
        "kbz",
        "kcx",
        "kcy",
        "kcz"
      ],
      "description": "cos(alpha) at ka between the tangent directions toward kb and kc (C13, C14, C17). sqrt(g) = sin(alpha) (C20) is recovered as sqrt(1 - cos^2), which is UNSIGNED; the paper's signed C16, sin(alpha) = k_hat . (e1 x e2), is positive everywhere on this grid (the (e1, e2, k_hat) basis is right-handed for the i-then-j ordering used here -- verified >= 0.875 over interior cells at every resolution and projection tested, with the two forms agreeing to 4e-16), so the unsigned sqrt form and C16 coincide and sqrt(g) is unambiguous.",
